  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Makerere University School of Public Health</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Each year in Uganda, nearly 46,000 newborns and 6,000 women die from problems during pregnancy and childbirth. Many of these deaths could be prevented if women used health services and if carers knew simple ways to keep newborns alive. This project will help families prepare for birth, provide better care to newborns, and know when to seek medical help. It is based on the lessons learned from two pilot projects that led to a big increase in the use of maternal health services and improved care of newborns. Men and community leaders will learn about the importance of maternal and newborn health. Pregnant women and their families will join savings groups to make sure they have the money they need during pregnancy and childbirth. Communities will find ways of getting affordable transport to the health centre for ante-natal care and childbirth. Community health workers will make home visits to pregnant women and those who have just given birth to check on the health of the mother and her baby. To improve the standard of health care, the project will train health workers, increase the number of midwives in project districts, buy equipment for maternal and newborn care, and provide incentives to improve health worker performance. As a result, the health of mothers and newborns in project areas will improve. Results will be shared to promote use of the approach in other districts.</narrative>
  </description>
